{% block main %}<div class="grid">
<div class="col-main">
<span class="kicker">Rutherford Reach Water Quality Dataset, v1.0</span>
<h1>Data</h1>
<p class="lede">Ten Saturdays at one station, in a schema that merges with agency data instead of sitting beside it.</p>
<p>Everything on this site is published as a table. One row per observation rather than per sampling event, so a row from this dock and a row from a state monitoring station can go in the same file without either being reshaped. The field names follow the Water Quality Exchange vocabulary that state and federal monitoring data already uses, and the download includes a crosswalk that says which project field maps to which WQX field, and where the mapping is imperfect.</p>

<h2>What is in it</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>Ten E. coli observations, 22 August to 24 October 2026, one per Saturday, with the plate counts behind each.</li>
<li>The seven field parameters for each of those Saturdays: water temperature, pH, turbidity, nitrate as N, orthophosphate, tide stage, and the 48 hour antecedent rainfall.</li>
<li>Three quality control rows, the split samples run by a certified laboratory, flagged as quality control rather than field samples so they do not double count.</li>
<li>The antecedent rainfall column, which is what makes the rain and risk model on the <a class='tx' href='/methods/#model'>methods page</a> reproducible from the file alone.</li>
</ul>
<p>That is 63 rows: ten Saturdays times six measured characteristics, plus the three laboratory splits. Tide stage and antecedent rainfall belong to the sampling event rather than to one measurement, so they ride on every row of that event.</p>

