tml#w03</td><td>so a single row can be checked without the file</td></tr><tr><td class="mono">license</td><td>text</td><td class="mono">CC BY 4.0</td><td>on every row</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The full field list, including the WQX mapping and the missing value convention for each field, is in the data dictionary that ships with the download.</p>

<h2>Download</h2>
<ul class="plain">
<li><a class="tx" href="/assets/data/rutherford-reach-wq-2026.csv">rutherford-reach-wq-2026.csv</a>, the 63 project rows, comma separated.</li>
<li><a class="tx" href="/assets/data/rutherford-reach-wq-2026.json">rutherford-reach-wq-2026.json</a>, the same rows as JSON, with the station block and the method description in the header.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both files are the project rows of v1.0, meaning every record I measured myself at this dock. The full v1.0 deposit also carries the imported agency and portal rows and is published as its own package with a datasheet, a data dictionary and a DOI.</p>
<p>Both files carry the same rows in the same order, and both are UTF-8 with no proprietary types. If they disagree, the CSV is the one to trust and I want to know about it.</p>

<h2>How to cite</h2>
<p class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding-left:16px; font-size:13px; line-height:1.6">Li, Haolin. <i>Rutherford Reach Water Quality Dataset</i>, v1.0. Passaic River Water Watch, 2026.<br>DOI: pending Zenodo deposit.</p>
<p>To cite one observation rather than the set, use its record id, for example RRWQ-2026-W03-ECOLI, which is stable and will not be reused if a value is ever corrected. A correction gets a new version of the file and a line in the changelog; the old rows stay where they are.</p>

<h2>License</h2>
<p>The data is released under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0. Use it, republish it, put it in a model, hand it to a class. Say where it came from.</p>

<h2>What I would want to know if I were downloading this</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>One station, one sampler, ten weeks. Nobody should fit anything ambitious to it on its own; it is most useful merged with the agency series for this reach, which is why the schema is what it is.</li>
<li>The E. coli values come from a field method, Coliscan Easygel, not from a certified laboratory. Three of the ten have a laboratory split beside them and they agree within 0.08 log10. The other seven do not have that check.</li>
<li>Every value is a multiple of 20 because of the plate arithmetic, not because of rounding.</li>
<li>The rainfall column is my own gauge at the boathouse. It is not a National Weather Service record and should not be merged with one without saying so.</li>
</ul>
